//? Title: Test serial correlation in linear-model residuals
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Detect serial dependence after fitting a trend

The example adds persistent Gaussian errors to a linear trend, fits the
trend, then examines the residuals in observation order. Durbin–Watson
compares successive residual differences with residual variation. Positive
correlation tends to make successive residuals closer together. Its
Monte Carlo reference simulates independent Gaussian errors in the
residual space of the fitted design.

Breusch–Godfrey adds lagged residuals to the fitted design and tests them
jointly. This example requests two lags and reports both the asymptotic
LM test and the approximate F test.

```{cpp, id=serial-correlation-tests, file="figures/serial_correlation_residuals.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/lm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    constexpr std::size_t count = 40;
    random::SplitMix64 innovations(42), calibration(731);
    Mat<> predictors(count, 1);
    std::vector<double> time, response;
    double state = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        state = .85 * state + innovations.normal();
        const double tick = static_cast<double>(i);
        time.push_back(tick);
        response.push_back(2 + .3 * tick + state);
        predictors(i, 0) = tick;
    }
    const stats::LM fit(response, predictors);
    const auto dw = fit.durbin_watson(calibration, 9999, stats::Alternative::Greater);
    const auto bg = fit.breusch_godfrey(2);
    const auto f = fit.breusch_godfrey(2, true);
    std::println("| Test | Statistic | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Durbin–Watson, positive correlation | {:.4f} | {:.3g} |", dw.statistic, dw.p_value);
    std::println("| Breusch–Godfrey LM, two lags | {:.4f} | {:.3g} |", bg.statistic, bg.p_value);
    std::println("| Breusch–Godfrey F, two lags | {:.4f} | {:.3g} |", f.statistic, f.p_value);

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    graphics::RPlot::create().line(time, fit.residuals(), "", {29, 78, 216}, 2.5)
        .abline_h(0, {100, 116, 139}, 1)
        .title("Residuals after fitting the trend").x_label("Observation").y_label("Residual")
        .size(740, 410).save_svg("build/serial_correlation_residuals.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Test | Statistic | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Durbin–Watson, positive correlation | 0.3271 | 0.0001 |
| Breusch–Godfrey LM, two lags | 27.0240 | 1.35e-06 |
| Breusch–Godfrey F, two lags | 37.4872 | 1.58e-09 |
\image(figures/serial_correlation_residuals.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Adjacent residuals stay on the same side of zero for several observations at a time.)
\alttext(A line chart shows long stretches of positive or negative residuals after a trend is fitted.)

The simulation uses the plus-one tail correction. With 9,999 null draws,
the smallest possible one-sided p-value is 0.0001.

